Here's a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::string line;
    std::istringstream is(markdown);

    while (std::getline(is, line)) {
        html += line;
    }

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdownText = "# Hello World\nThis is a sample of markdown text.";
    std::string htmlText = markdownToHtml(markdownText);
    std::cout << "Markdown Text: " << markdownText << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML Output: " << htmlText << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line and appends it to a `std::string` called `html`. This approach ensures that the markdown text is converted to HTML without any special formatting or escaping. The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHtml` function by converting a sample markdown text to HTML text.

